---
import { versioned } from '../data/asset-version';
/**
 * Confirmation Outlook — a dedicated top-level page, on the main-street.astro
 * pattern (MarketingLayout + page-local sections + inline schema).
 *
 * Since D29 this is PROOF of what gets built at scale, not an offer — the
 * rendered word "flagship" retired. The page, its content, and its honesty
 * contract are unchanged by that.
 *
 * NOT /work/<slug>/ and NOT a demos.ts row, deliberately:
 *   - DemoLayout carries a "← Work" backlink and a "Live Demo" pill, while the
 *     page would be absent from the /work/ gallery (which builds from demos.ts).
 *     That reads as a bug.
 *   - demoSchema() asserts `isAccessibleForFree` and `operatingSystem: 'Web
 *     browser'`. Both are false of a rate-limited hosted Python service.
 *   - proofStats[0] says "Open any one in your browser" — true of the seven,
 *     false of this. The count and the claim are welded; adding an 8th row
 *     would make one of them a lie.
 *
 * All copy reads from src/data/flagship.ts, which carries the honesty contract:
 * the engine is measured frequencies over observed transitions — never call it
 * ML, a model, trained, or a forecast.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { flagship } from '../data/flagship';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;
const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    '@id': `${origin}/confirmation-outlook/#app`,
    name: flagship.name,
    applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
    url: flagship.cta.href,
    author: { '@id': `${origin}/#organization` },
    description: flagship.body,
    // Deliberately NOT asserting isAccessibleForFree or operatingSystem — the
    // service is rate-limited and is not a browser-local app. See the note above.
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${origin}/` },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: flagship.name,
        item: `${origin}/confirmation-outlook/`,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---
<MarketingLayout
  schema={schema}
  title={`${flagship.name} — a live service`}
  {/* NOT flagship.body, which is 298 characters. That copy is written to be READ
     on the page; a search snippet is cut off well before its end, so the last
     clause reached nobody. The page still renders the full body. */}
  description="A supply-chain risk service built end to end and deployed: it reports measured frequencies over observed transitions, and never calls that a forecast."
  image="/og/confirmation-outlook.png"
>
  {/* ── Hero — claim, CTAs, then the proof ───────────────────────────────
      D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE, 2026-09-13. This page ran four bands at
      max-w-4xl — one width, to the pixel, for the whole scroll — with three of
      the four on canvas and only the middle one painting a ground. That is the
      complaint D113 was raised on, and the widest a page can state it: nothing
      at all separated the hero from the fact strip under it but a hairline.

      It now walks content, feature, content, popout for the column and canvas,
      deep, surface, canvas for the ground, with the rhythm running regular,
      tight, regular, tight under both. Every neighbor differs in all three, and
      the palette does none of the work — a hue is the lightest signal on a
      page, which is the argument src/components/kit/industrial.js makes under
      WHY THREE AND NOT SEVEN.

      THE HERO STOPS AT `content` BECAUSE OF THE SCREENSHOT, and this is the one
      band on the page whose width was decided by something other than reading.
      The console capture is a committed 1200px-wide file and it renders at
      `w-full`, so a `feature` column (84rem, 1344px) would scale it up by an
      eighth and soften the one image that has to look like real software.
      `content` is 72rem — 1152px, under the source width, so the picture is
      never asked to be bigger than it is. Regenerating it to suit a band is
      not on the table: that needs ART_VERSION bumped and it is not what this
      pass is for.

      THE FACT STRIP IS THE BAND THAT CHANGED MOST, and it earned it. Four
      numbers reading 2,400 materials and 53k order lines are the only thing on
      the page a reader can weigh in a glance, and they were set in the same
      column as the prose above and below them with a hairline at each end. It
      is now the widest column and the only deep ground on the page, at the
      shortest rhythm — a plinth under the hero rather than a fourth paragraph.

      NOTHING MOVED AND NOTHING WAS CUT. The header above argues why this page
      is not a demos.ts row and what the copy may never say about the engine,
      and the CTA block carries its own argument for sitting high. Both are
      where they were written. Reshaping is not reordering. */}
  <Section width="content" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
        <p class="mono-label inline-flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="status-dot"></span>
          {flagship.eyebrow}
        </p>
        <span class="mono-label text-ink-faint">{flagship.host}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="statement mt-5">
        {flagship.name}
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xl leading-relaxed">
        <span class="aiq-accent">{flagship.tagline}</span>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{flagship.body}</p>

      {/* CTAs sit HIGH, directly under the pitch — the one thing this page
          exists to drive must never fall below the fold. */}
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <Button href={flagship.cta.href} variant="primary">{flagship.cta.label}</Button>
        <Button href={flagship.source.href} variant="secondary">{flagship.source.label}</Button>
      </div>

      {/* Static by construction: a committed screenshot, not an iframe or an
          uptime fetch. This page must render perfectly from the edge even when
          the demo box is dark. `.status-dot` is a pure-CSS pulse and asserts
          nothing about uptime. */}
      <img
        src={versioned("/shots/confirmation-outlook.webp")}
        width="1200"
        height="630"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        alt="The Confirmation Outlook operator console — a risk register of orders ranked by probability of missing their confirmed date."
        class="mt-10 w-full rounded-xl border border-line shadow-2xl shadow-black/40"
      />
  </Section>

  {/* ── Fact strip — 4-up, and now the plinth the hero stands on ──────────
       THE OLD NOTE STILL HOLDS AND IS WHY THIS BAND CAN GO WIDER. The Opener
       panel ran these 2-up because it was 5/12 of a grid and four mono labels
       collided at that width; max-w-4xl freed it to four, and `feature` is the
       same argument taken one step — the labels have room to breathe rather
       than merely to fit.

       THE DEEP GROUND AND THE SHORTEST RHYTHM ARE THE OTHER TWO SIGNALS. Four
       measured numbers under a claim is the one place on this page a reader
       weighs something rather than reads it, and a band it can be told apart
       from is what makes that possible. `border-b border-line` came off with
       the conversion: `.section-deep` paints its own hairline top and bottom as
       inset box-shadows, so the utility was a second rule over the class's own.

       `--color-ink-faint` IS REMAPPED INSIDE THIS GROUND AND THAT MATTERS HERE.
       global.css replaces it with ink-muted on `.section-deep` because faint
       measures 4.37:1 on this surface against a 4.5 floor — and `dt` below is
       the first faint metadata anywhere to land on that ground, which is the
       case the remap was written for before anything rendered it. */}
  <Section tone="deep" width="feature" density="tight">
    <dl class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-5 sm:grid-cols-4">
      {flagship.facts.map((f) => (
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
          <dd class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">{f.v}</dd>
          <dt class="mono-label text-ink-faint">{f.k}</dt>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </Section>

  {/* ── How it's built ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Back up to the surface ground, in from the widest column to the reading
       one, and the standard rhythm returns — three signals against the strip
       above. The three panels get more room than they had, not less: max-w-4xl
       was 896px for a `md:grid-cols-3` grid, and `content` is 1152px.

       THE NUMERAL IS GONE. PICKUP.md scores a decorative "/ 01" as a
       generated-design signal, and this one numbered a sequence of exactly
       itself — no band on this page carried an 02. The label it sat beside is
       untouched and now stands on its own, which is what every reshaped page
       does with its eyebrow. */}
  <Section tone="surface" width="content">
      <p class="mono-label">How it’s built</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        The restraint is the point.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Anyone can bolt a model onto a dashboard and call the output a forecast. This one only ever
        tells you what it actually measured — and refuses to ship if the numbers don’t hold.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
        {flagship.arch.map((a) => (
          <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-2 p-6">
            <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-ink">{a.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{a.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="mono-label mt-8 text-ink-faint">{flagship.honesty}</p>
  </Section>

  {/* ── Closing CTA ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The narrowest column and the shortest rhythm on the page, back on
       canvas. Three signals against the band above, and the column is the one
       doing the work: this is a heading, a paragraph and two buttons addressed
       to one reader, and a measure they can hold is worth more to it than the
       room the panels above needed. */}
  <Section width="popout" density="tight" class="text-center">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
      This is what I ship. Yours would be built the same way.
    </h2>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Built end to end for a fictional manufacturer — engine, API, and console. Tell me the problem
      your operation is actually carrying, and I’ll tell you straight how I’d build the fix.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
      <Button href="/contact/?intent=quote" variant="primary">Start a project</Button>
      <Button href={flagship.cta.href} variant="secondary">{flagship.cta.label}</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>
</MarketingLayout>
